19 0 obj
<< /A 57 0 R /Alt (\(a\) Leaf with small brown spots surrounded by yellow halos. \(b\) Leaf with light brown blotches scattered randomly. \(c\) Leaf heavily covered with dark brown spots. \(d\) Leaf with numerous dark brown spots, more frequent than \(c\). \(e\) Yellow leaf indicating discoloration. \(f\) Healthy green leaf with minimal blemishes.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 58 0 R /Alt (Image displays six variations of the same plant. The original image on the left shows a green leafy plant on soil. Arrows point to five altered images labeled: Horizontal flip, Vertical flip, Random 90-degree rotation, Random small rotation, Random exposure, and Random shear. Each variant demonstrates a different transformation technique applied to the original image.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 59 0 R /Alt (Flowchart of a convolutional neural network architecture for leaf disease detection. It includes sections labeled as Backbone and Neck, with functions like Convolution \(Conv\), Bottleneck, Concatenation \(Concat\), and Detection. An input image of leaves, identified as 640x640x3, goes through layers such as C3K2 and LAE. The process results in a similar-sized output image with highlighted detection areas.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 60 0 R /Alt (Diagram depicting a dual-extraction process for image processing. The upper section titled \215Lightweight Extraction\216 shows input data split into groups for dimension mapping, using a rearrange operator and simplifying with a multiplication step. The lower section, \215Adaptive Extraction,\216 involves average pooling, convolution, and softmax operations to further process data. Both sections contribute to a combined output. Color-coded symbols represent key operations: average pooling \(AP\), convolution \(Conv\), rearrange operator \(R\), and softmax \(S\). The final output is shown as having reduced dimensions.) /K 1 /P 9 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 61 0 R /Alt (Diagram of an attention mechanism with two modules. The upper section illustrates the Channel Attention Module, where input features undergo max pooling and average pooling, pass through a shared MLP, and merge into channel attention. The lower section shows the Spatial Attention Module, where channel-refined features pass through a convolution layer and max-average pooled, leading to spatial attention.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 62 0 R /Alt (Diagram of a neural network architecture with a backbone layer feeding three branches. Each branch consists of interconnected panels leading to a prediction step. Arrows indicate data flow from the backbone through intermediary stages to the final prediction output. Orange lines represent connections between layers within each branch.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 63 0 R /Alt (A single-board computer with a large fan on top, multiple USB ports, and two cameras connected via ribbon cables. The setup includes visible HDMI ports and an Ethernet port.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Four line graphs comparing YOLO-PLNet and YOLOv1n over 200 epochs. Top left graph shows precision; top right, recall; bottom left, mAP@0.5; bottom right, mAP@0.5:0.95. YOLO-PLNet outperforms YOLOv1n in all metrics, especially noticeable in the zoomed inset sections from epochs 160 to 200.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 48 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (A grid of plant leaf images depicting four rows labeled A to D and multiple columns. Row A shows original images of leaves with different conditions. Row B uses YOLO11n, highlighting areas of interest with colored boxes and labels indicating issues like early rust or pests. Row C uses YOLO-PL.net, also showcasing detected problems with similar annotations. Row D displays heatmap visualizations focusing on areas of interest in varying intensities of color, indicating problem severity on the leaves.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 66 0 R /Alt (Precision-recall curve displaying the performance of multiple classes: Early Leaf Spot, Early Rust, Late Leaf Spot, Nutrition Deficiency, Rust, and Healthy, with precision on the Y-axis and recall on the X-axis. Each class line is color-coded. All classes combined have a mean average precision of 0.968 at a threshold of 0.5.) /K 1 /P 9 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
29 0 obj
<< /A 67 0 R /Alt (Normalized confusion matrix visualizing classification performance across categories: Early Leaf Spot, Early Rust, Late Leaf Spot, Nutrition Deficiency, Rust, Healthy, and Background. Values range from 0 to 1, with dark blue indicating higher accuracy. Each category has varying degrees of prediction accuracy, with Early Rust achieving perfect classification.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 50 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
30 0 obj
<< /A 68 0 R /Alt (Flowchart showing the process of deploying the YOLO-PLnet model on an edge device, Jetson Orin NX. The model is optimized using the TensorRT inference framework. The process involves building the TensorRT engine, deserializing the engine, and performing inference, with results displayed on a computer screen.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 52 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
31 0 obj
<< /A 69 0 R /Alt (Six images of plant leaves showing signs of disease are labeled as frames 17, 63, 162, 230, 309, and 550. Each frame contains leaves with different colored boxes indicating disease classification: red for class 0, pink for class 1, green for class 2, orange for class 3, blue for class 4, and black for class 5. Each image highlights specific affected areas on the leaves with colored boxes.) /K 0 /P 9 0 R /Pg 53 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
